WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.193 --> 00:00:03.110
(electronic music)

00:00:05.530 --> 00:00:07.220
- When I went through
the surgery for Moyamoya,

00:00:07.220 --> 00:00:10.553
I didn't tell anyone.
(bright music)

00:00:15.810 --> 00:00:17.754
It was a very difficult time for me.

00:00:17.754 --> 00:00:19.670
(electronic music)

00:00:19.670 --> 00:00:23.160
I had to write down what would happen

00:00:23.160 --> 00:00:25.984
to all my things if I were to die.

00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:29.600
(electronic music)

00:00:29.600 --> 00:00:32.070
I think when you're faced
with your own mortality,

00:00:32.070 --> 00:00:33.840
you wanna make sure
that you lived your life

00:00:33.840 --> 00:00:35.140
how you wanted to live it.

00:00:36.550 --> 00:00:38.440
I just wanna keep making
the most amazing music

00:00:38.440 --> 00:00:42.157
and showing people that if I
can do it, anyone can do it.

00:00:42.157 --> 00:00:45.892
(electronic music)

00:00:45.892 --> 00:00:48.475
(upbeat music)

00:00:50.510 --> 00:00:52.930
My name is Jennifer Lee,
but I'm better known

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:54.565
as TOKiMONSTA.
♪ Show me ♪

00:00:54.565 --> 00:00:58.429
♪ Where the light stays ♪

00:00:58.429 --> 00:01:01.000
♪ Find it on the path ♪
I am a music producer

00:01:01.000 --> 00:01:06.000
and live performer DJ, and all
the songs are composed by me.

00:01:07.130 --> 00:01:09.920
Electronic music is the
music producer's world.

00:01:09.920 --> 00:01:12.843
It is what you can do
to push the envelope.

00:01:13.903 --> 00:01:16.698
♪ I ♪
My music is electronic.

00:01:16.698 --> 00:01:19.250
♪ In your feelings ♪

00:01:19.250 --> 00:01:23.730
But is has many organic
sounds, like a piano,

00:01:23.730 --> 00:01:27.402
or a violin. How you guys doing?

00:01:27.402 --> 00:01:30.330
Going a step further beyond
that is creating instruments

00:01:30.330 --> 00:01:34.553
out of non-instruments.
("Rouge" by Tokimonsta)

00:01:43.340 --> 00:01:46.749
So something that I like to
do is use field recordings.

00:01:46.749 --> 00:01:48.860
(bright music)

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:51.170
I take a little recorder around with me

00:01:51.170 --> 00:01:54.492
and record different kinds
of sounds that I like.

00:01:54.492 --> 00:01:57.480
(helicopter roars)
(bright music)

00:01:57.480 --> 00:02:00.623
I can use little bits of
that to create new sounds.

00:02:00.623 --> 00:02:03.768
(bright music)
(sticks click)

00:02:03.768 --> 00:02:06.255
(foot scoffs)
(bright music)

00:02:06.255 --> 00:02:08.150
(rock dings)
(bright music)

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:10.670
They do have a really interesting tonality

00:02:10.670 --> 00:02:14.903
that is not common in music.
(bright music)

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:19.330
This is my song, "Rose's Thorn."

00:02:19.330 --> 00:02:23.312
It is a track off my most
recent album, Lune Rouge.

00:02:23.312 --> 00:02:27.229
("Rose's Thorn" by Tokimonsta)

00:02:31.089 --> 00:02:33.800
With this track, I did use
a lot of field recordings.

00:02:33.800 --> 00:02:37.727
For the drum sounds, I
decided to use a truck door.

00:02:37.727 --> 00:02:39.020
(truck door slams)

00:02:39.020 --> 00:02:41.490
Rocks hitting each other.
(rocks click)

00:02:41.490 --> 00:02:44.900
And a regular snare drum.
(snare drum clacks)

00:02:44.900 --> 00:02:46.660
I layered them all on top of each other.

00:02:46.660 --> 00:02:49.312
Together, we have--

00:02:49.312 --> 00:02:50.674
(percussion clacks)

00:02:50.674 --> 00:02:52.560
And the great thing is when
you do stuff like that,

00:02:52.560 --> 00:02:54.360
no one else will have that sound.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:57.430
No one else will have a
snare-truck-door-rock sound

00:02:57.430 --> 00:03:01.653
in their song.
(upbeat percussion music)

00:03:02.550 --> 00:03:03.960
They're unusual sounds,

00:03:03.960 --> 00:03:05.780
things that are not considered music.

00:03:05.780 --> 00:03:06.880
I recorded

00:03:07.770 --> 00:03:11.900
this wonderful beach sound.
(ocean waves gush)

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.405
That like weird, bird chirping.

00:03:14.405 --> 00:03:17.540
(birds chirp)

00:03:17.540 --> 00:03:21.810
There is something to me
about creating an environment

00:03:21.810 --> 00:03:24.790
in a song, it takes you somewhere else,

00:03:24.790 --> 00:03:27.250
beyond just the song and the melodies,

00:03:27.250 --> 00:03:30.380
you're actually removing
someone from their environment

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:33.476
and putting them by the
sea, putting them in nature.

00:03:33.476 --> 00:03:36.393
(ocean waves gush)

00:03:37.462 --> 00:03:40.320
(bright music)

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:42.803
I was raised in Torrance, California.

00:03:43.700 --> 00:03:45.993
I was born to an immigrant mother.

00:03:47.370 --> 00:03:49.050
My father passed away
when I was very young,

00:03:49.050 --> 00:03:51.620
so for the most part, I was
raised by a single mother

00:03:51.620 --> 00:03:53.993
who worked very hard to
kind of provide for me.

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:57.840
So I took piano for about 10 years,

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:01.380
and my upbringing introduced me to music.

00:04:01.380 --> 00:04:02.950
Though, I don't think
my mom intended for me

00:04:02.950 --> 00:04:07.010
to become a musician.
(upbeat music)

00:04:07.010 --> 00:04:08.530
I remember

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:10.210
discovering

00:04:10.210 --> 00:04:12.710
west coast hip-hop, everything about it

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:14.480
was something that appealed to me a lot,

00:04:14.480 --> 00:04:17.600
and I flourished in that.
(upbeat music)

00:04:17.600 --> 00:04:19.820
I would start producing
once I entered college.

00:04:19.820 --> 00:04:22.740
Even though I loved hip-hop,
I had no access to rappers

00:04:22.740 --> 00:04:25.080
when I was starting out,
so the music started

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.973
to become hip-hop beats but
with electronic sounds in it.

00:04:30.270 --> 00:04:32.700
No one had heard this style of music

00:04:32.700 --> 00:04:35.460
that was so much hip-hop
but was so forward thinking

00:04:35.460 --> 00:04:39.110
and electronic. I started participating

00:04:39.110 --> 00:04:41.340
in other events with other producers.

00:04:41.340 --> 00:04:44.181
And I became apart of a scene.

00:04:44.181 --> 00:04:47.681
(upbeat percussion music)

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:53.500
That itself could almost
just be like a rap beat.

00:04:53.500 --> 00:04:55.130
The drums themselves are just so simple

00:04:55.130 --> 00:04:58.120
without anything else,
that's why I find it.

00:04:58.120 --> 00:04:59.570
Percussion is very important.

00:04:59.570 --> 00:05:04.093
I think it creates movement.
(upbeat percussion music)

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.979
Even the pianos, the main
thing is that there's movement.

00:05:07.979 --> 00:05:10.562
(upbeat music)

00:05:12.530 --> 00:05:15.500
That bass sound is very simple.

00:05:15.500 --> 00:05:19.070
All it is is one note.
(upbeat music)

00:05:19.070 --> 00:05:22.363
Rhythm guitar.
(upbeat music)

00:05:24.340 --> 00:05:27.543
The guitar lead.
(upbeat music)

00:05:28.570 --> 00:05:30.200
They are not coming from a real guitar.

00:05:30.200 --> 00:05:33.140
I was going for a
non-realistic guitar sound.

00:05:33.140 --> 00:05:35.140
I made this song like
right after my surgery.

00:05:35.140 --> 00:05:37.133
It was one of the first
songs that I worked on.

00:05:37.133 --> 00:05:40.966
(dramatic, atmospheric music)

00:05:46.540 --> 00:05:49.293
Around 10 years ago, I
started getting migraines.

00:05:50.400 --> 00:05:54.610
One doctor suggested that I
potentially could have a disease

00:05:54.610 --> 00:05:57.900
called Moyamoya, the arteries in the left

00:05:57.900 --> 00:06:00.340
and right side that
supply blood to your brain

00:06:00.340 --> 00:06:02.280
start to shrink, and then,
so what ends up happening

00:06:02.280 --> 00:06:05.590
is stroke, aneurism, you will die.

00:06:05.590 --> 00:06:08.460
Fast forward about 10 years later,

00:06:08.460 --> 00:06:11.460
I had a strange incident where
I couldn't feel my left foot.

00:06:12.390 --> 00:06:13.280
In the back of my head,

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:15.380
I've always had that Moyamoya thing there.

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:20.090
By January of 2015, I
had two brain surgeries

00:06:20.090 --> 00:06:21.957
a week apart from each other.

00:06:21.957 --> 00:06:24.200
(dramatic, atmospheric music)

00:06:24.200 --> 00:06:27.680
Right after the surgery,
I was tired and in pain,

00:06:27.680 --> 00:06:29.500
but I was OK.

00:06:29.500 --> 00:06:32.190
It was the recovery
process that was difficult.

00:06:32.190 --> 00:06:34.870
I would get stroke symptoms
where I couldn't feel

00:06:34.870 --> 00:06:38.300
the entire left side of my
body and, just out of nowhere,

00:06:38.300 --> 00:06:40.400
I completely lost my ability to talk

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.740
and to understand speech.

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.090
And I could no longer
communicate with anyone,

00:06:44.090 --> 00:06:46.330
and one thing that I noticed
about this entire process

00:06:46.330 --> 00:06:48.653
was I couldn't understand music anymore.

00:06:50.730 --> 00:06:52.243
It's nothing but noise.

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.315
Like an airplane flying in the sky.

00:06:55.315 --> 00:06:57.690
(airplane roars)
I couldn't understand music,

00:06:57.690 --> 00:06:59.190
so there was no music anymore.

00:07:01.460 --> 00:07:04.935
That was very difficult for me.

00:07:04.935 --> 00:07:07.768
(uplifting music)

00:07:10.920 --> 00:07:13.500
Eventually, I noticed that
the language was getting

00:07:13.500 --> 00:07:15.740
a little better, day by day.

00:07:15.740 --> 00:07:18.560
A soundtrack was coming
back, all the music

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.380
that you hear, in the
elevator, out of people's cars,

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:23.210
on TV--
- Understanding--

00:07:23.210 --> 00:07:25.810
- All these things were slowly
creeping back into my life,

00:07:25.810 --> 00:07:28.719
and I was elated.
(bright music)

00:07:28.719 --> 00:07:30.382
- Hi.
- Hey.

00:07:30.382 --> 00:07:32.230
You look good.
- How's it going?

00:07:32.230 --> 00:07:33.896
- [Tokimonsta] Good.

00:07:33.896 --> 00:07:34.950
- [Ioanna] What are you doing today?

00:07:34.950 --> 00:07:37.360
- So this is what I have up right now.

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:39.460
These are all from the same take,

00:07:39.460 --> 00:07:41.280
but I had to mix them all differently

00:07:41.280 --> 00:07:44.030
'cause depending on what note you sing,

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:46.770
those frequencies would jump out more.

00:07:46.770 --> 00:07:48.837
When I went to open my
computer to be like,

00:07:48.837 --> 00:07:50.697
"OK, this is the time,
it's my time to shine.

00:07:50.697 --> 00:07:52.397
"I'm gonna try making a new song."

00:07:53.280 --> 00:07:56.220
It was awful, it was garbage,
it didn't sound good at all.

00:07:56.220 --> 00:07:58.690
I didn't know how to make music anymore.

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.190
I figured if everything else came back,

00:08:00.190 --> 00:08:02.770
this would come back.
- Let's try re-recording

00:08:02.770 --> 00:08:05.550
one of your leads for our song.

00:08:05.550 --> 00:08:07.360
- Cool.
- And see if we can

00:08:07.360 --> 00:08:09.973
like breathe in some new life.
- Sounds good.

00:08:10.900 --> 00:08:12.820
- I'm gonna start from the--
- People think I had

00:08:12.820 --> 00:08:15.730
to relearn how to make music;
I didn't have to relearn it.

00:08:15.730 --> 00:08:17.150
I just had to wait for it to come back.

00:08:17.150 --> 00:08:18.350
- [Ioanna] But I'll
play like a couple bars

00:08:18.350 --> 00:08:21.140
before it goes in.
- Fast forward

00:08:21.140 --> 00:08:24.590
just like a week or two
weeks, I was able to make

00:08:24.590 --> 00:08:27.950
the very first song that ended
up on my most recent album.

00:08:27.950 --> 00:08:28.783
Cool!

00:08:29.670 --> 00:08:31.584
That was great.
- Cool!

00:08:31.584 --> 00:08:32.417
- What a great recording.

00:08:32.417 --> 00:08:34.800
Another element of the song
are these little vocal samples.

00:08:34.800 --> 00:08:37.810
They are not words, they
don't mean anything.

00:08:37.810 --> 00:08:40.059
It's just effects.

00:08:40.059 --> 00:08:42.642
(singers scat)

00:08:47.300 --> 00:08:49.840
It is having the beauty

00:08:50.770 --> 00:08:53.270
of a person's voice

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:56.100
without them distracting you with words,

00:08:56.100 --> 00:08:58.810
and these songs essentially
make themselves.

00:08:58.810 --> 00:09:02.990
And, even I'm surprised at
the end how they come to feel.

00:09:02.990 --> 00:09:05.850
Like I had no idea I'd end
up making a song like this.

00:09:05.850 --> 00:09:08.350
For me, as a musician,
I always wanna innovate.

00:09:08.350 --> 00:09:10.290
I don't wanna be making
the same type of music

00:09:10.290 --> 00:09:12.520
as everyone else, 'cause
then, why would people

00:09:12.520 --> 00:09:17.520
need to listen to my music?
("Rose's Thorn" by Tokimonsta)

00:09:30.620 --> 00:09:33.150
After the brain surgery,
the one thing that changed

00:09:33.150 --> 00:09:36.340
was my philosophical approach.

00:09:36.340 --> 00:09:39.210
Once you're faced with the
fact that you might die,

00:09:39.210 --> 00:09:40.810
you realize, I don't wanna live my life

00:09:40.810 --> 00:09:42.960
for anyone else but myself.

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:46.410
I wanna be the kind of artist
that creates her own vision

00:09:46.410 --> 00:09:49.260
for herself, no matter
what it is that I do.

00:09:49.260 --> 00:09:52.414
And I wanna know that each
day I live, I feel fulfilled.

00:09:52.414 --> 00:09:56.331
("Rose's Thorn" by Tokimonsta)

